A||||9||86se/lambo||After R -134 conversion||||9||
Z||000000||86se/lambo||05-07-2000||03:00 AM||||After R-134 conversion, i noticed the cooling fan stays on constantly now and the a/c clutch cycles often ( every 20-30 seconds). I dont mind the fan much, but the compressor bothers me. It does get cold.<BR>I changed out the compressor,accumulator,expansion valve and flushed the lines. Anyway any suggestions ?||||reg||9
Z||000001||Phil||05-07-2000||06:11 AM||||I gather you did the conversion yourself, if so it sounds like you may have an undercharge. Get a cheapie gage (Pep Boys $10) and check the pressure against the values in the chart that comes with the gage. The fan is supposed to run all the time when the A/C is on. If all else fails see a pro.||||reg||1
Z||000002||86se/lambo||06-24-2000||09:12 PM||||well i did find the answer, just forgot to reply. when i did the compressor conversion i attatched the wrong wire to the compressor(even though it had the proper connector).<BR>this was a v5 compressor stock to a da6 compressor. after iconnected the correct wire the fan worked properly but the a/c compressor still cycled tooo frecquently. i removed the wire connector at the low pressure switch at the accumalator and turned the cycling screw one quarter counterclockwise and that did it . ||||reg||10
Z||000003||84 Indy||06-24-2000||11:06 PM||||How much did the conversion cost?  And what was needed?  Part numbers?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||06-25-2000||09:54 AM||||According to my mechanic, it's a fairly expensive deal mostly because you're supposed to change the compressor.  He said it was a few hundred just for that. (maybe for all of the parts?)  He said that most of the time he doesn't recommend it unless you have to get new parts anyway.  The only real advantage I can see is that R134a costs alot less than R12 and R12 isn't manufactured anymore.  If you don't have any leaks, you shouldn't have to recharge your system anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||06-25-2000||02:05 PM||||The only reason to change the compressor when converting to R-134 would be if the compressor was bad.  If all the components in the system were good and the only reason to convert to 134 is because the R-12 leaked out, the only part that needs to be replaced is the reciever/dryer.  Most folks change the orifice tube also since it is prone to being clogged and only costs around $10.  The compressor should be removed to drain the oil out of it, but it can be reused. I did the conversion on my 84 for a total of around $120 for the conversion kit, reciever/dryer, and orifice tube. I did all the work myself.  It's been 3 years and it still cools great!  Icicles on demand <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||jlruiz67||06-25-2000||02:38 PM||||I must have made a big mistake. I got a conversion kit from PepBoys for arownd $15 and it included a new fitting, the oil and the new R-134, my system was drained due to leaks and I purchased a sealer for the leak. I changed one fitting added the oil, R-134 and the sealer and that's all I did. I also purchased a pressure gage for about $5. I is really cold inside my fiero now and it all works great for arround $20. You see I must have made a mistake because you all are talking of hundreds of dollars and I spent only $20. Did I do something wrong here. It all works great. As far as I know the onlything needed is to empty out our system and change one fitting that is provided in the kit and fill it backup. I hope this helps someone.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rodrv6||06-25-2000||06:15 PM||||The reciever/dryer is in the system to remove any moisture that may be present.  If the system has been open to the outside air for any length of time, the dessicant inside the reciever/dryer will become saturated with moisture and needs to be changed.  This can also happen if the system has a leak and has lost it's refrigerant charge.  It's also not a bad idea to replace it anytime the system is going to be recharged if it is over five years old or so.  Moisture in the system will react with the refrigerant over time and form an acid compound that is bad for the components.<BR>I'm a little curious how anyone can successfully recharge an empty system with only a pressure gauge.  Any A/C system that has lost a charge due to a leak MUST be evacuated for an hour or so with a vacuum pump to remove any moisture present.  If not, you WILL have trouble down the road.  The "cheapie" conversions may work for a while, but they can be very expensive in the long run.||||reg||4
Z||000008||jlruiz67||06-26-2000||12:03 AM||||Well I checked the pressure of the system and found  0 pressure, so I then got the retrofit kit from PepBoys and started filling as desctibed above. I know that I could have needed to evacuate the system or replaced parts but the experiment was only about $20 to see if it would work and if it didn't it would be ok because I would know that it wasn't someting symple, yes I did this only with a pressure gage (a cheap one at that) And just so you know it has worked great for the past 7 months. Now here in So. California its pretty hot and I'm glad to have the good old AC.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tom88gt||06-28-2000||12:44 PM||||My conversion was also cheap and still works great.  My system was completely empty.  I vacummed the system for an hour.  Vacumm pump cost $15 and runs off my air compressor.  But you could probably skip this part.  Added new low side valve.  added R-134 and oil.  Nice and cold.  You can use a guage to check system pressure but just using the cans provided in the kit will get you in the ballpark.  Plus the high pressure relief valve will prevent damage from over filling.  My high pressure cutout switch port had a leak so I filled it a couple of times before I found the pin hole and fixed it.  You can just pull the switch to drain any old R-12 oil.  I didn't replace the drier but may do so later on if the AC drops off.  They are $48, as I remember, at my local parts place.  You may even be able to remove the drier and cook it in your oven overnight on low/100-200' to remove the moisture from the desicant.  <P>Don't be fooled by shops quoting you $$$.  It's their business to do inexpensive repairs for $$$.  Ever notice that when you have a truly indepth repair, they say you're better off getting rid of the car or that they are too busy to do it?  They want easy work.||||reg||7
